  "description": "q-degree of the six NF coefficients of hecke_core_symbolic.build_atom_nf_sym(Z, N), measured exactly for every nucleus to A=30 via a Fraction-exact Laurent mirror gated against the real function. FINDINGS. (a) The coefficients are Laurent polynomials in Z[1/2][q,q^-1] -- NOT the 'Q(q, sqrt q)' the docstring claims; the sqrt q was a convention-C artefact and z never enters hm_sym. (b) Support is exactly symmetric, lo = -hi, in every component of every nucleus tested. (c) A Z/2 grading: exponent parity + NF word length is constant per nucleus, so each coefficient is a +-1 eigenvector of the bar involution q -> 1/q with the sign fixed by word length. (d) Degree is QUADRATIC in A, from the A(A-1)/2 pair steps. On the all-proton ray deg(sigma0) = 12A + A(A-1)/2 - 1 exactly for A=1..30, one less than the number of hm_sym calls: pp pairs carry d=0 and nothing cancels. On the pure-neutron ray deg(gamma) = A(A+1)/2 on 7<=A<=26 and BREAKS at A=27 -- scoped observation, not a law; a sweep stopping at A=16 would have called it exact. (e) The max-plus bound on the effective step matrix is a genuine upper bound but is attained in under a third of components; the gap is always even, zero on the proton-rich side, and reaches 104 in degree at A=12 (the largest in this sweep, and still growing there -- a floor, not a max), so neutron-rich coefficients cancel at leading order in a way no degree-only model sees. NOT ESTABLISHED: no mechanism for that cancellation or for the A=27 break. shift_NF = 18Z+21N+1 is a DIFFERENT quantity (deuteron 40 vs deg(sigma0) 23) and is not reconciled here.",
